{"kind":"report","text":"CHANGES-REQUESTED at exact pushed head ebb4dceb7fbe5b4d422fedc85bb982522500247d (origin/request/jsonata-spike-three-repair, one commit on rejected base 4b93b6d8, Rests-On child request 95bdf9c7), review request #15482 against child request 95bdf9c7, parent d719428f, verdict 168b6845, and the six cited artifacts. The two named blockers are mechanically satisfied — both mutation checks reproduce and every gate is green — but two conditions of this review are not met.\n\nBlocker findings, both reproduced independently:\n\n1. The nested-duplicate refusal demanded by Blocker 1 has no test. The only duplicate regression is top-level (inventory_ui_test.go:134), and it PASSES with the submission-boundary canonicalization removed — it is protected by decodeEvent's downstream bytes-equality check, not by the canonicalizer, so the recursive branch of decodeUniqueJSONValue (inventory_ui.go:319-322) has no red proof. The review condition requires the recursive duplicate check to hold; an untested guard branch in exactly the code this repair adds does not meet the project's map-each-guard-to-its-mutation-test bar. Fix: add a nested-duplicate payload regression (and ideally one that distinguishes the boundary canonicalizer from the downstream check).\n\n2. CORPUS.md still claims a category exclusion the guard does not deliver — the same class of untruth Blocker 2 was filed to repair. Lines 30-32 state profile loading rejects \"object wildcard or descendant traversal\", but the profile ADMITS `(*)`, `*[0]`, `**[0]`, and `($x := *; $x)`; I demonstrated `(*)` and `*[0]` are genuinely order-dependent in the Go port (two distinct results across 64 runs). Parenthesizing the exact expression this commit rejects re-admits it. The request's own condition says a narrowed implementation \"must still account explicitly for the admitted order-dependent expressions and may not claim the full exclusion.\" Fix: either reject `*` path-tokens via the compiled AST rather than regex spelling, or narrow the CORPUS.md sentence to the rejected spellings and record the admitted evasions explicitly.\n\nWhat is sound and should be preserved unchanged in the repair:\n\n- Architecture: spike layer only (spike/jsonataddl, six paths); no kernel, host, or production path touched; the documented spike contract is otherwise preserved and needs no architecture-doc update.\n- Blocker 1 mechanics: canonicalEventPayload (inventory_ui.go:272-296) runs after credential/envelope/type/idempotency checks and before decodeEvent and the signing Append, so signed bytes are canonical bytes; raw 16KiB bound, UTF-8, object-type, trailing-data refusals all present; the browser-flow test (inventory_ui_test.go:24) submits the literal unsorted raw string verbatim through all four UI steps; removing the canonicalization fails it with HTTP 400 (reproduced) and restore is green.\n- Blocker 2 mechanics: the regex alternative rejects bare * and leading **.id; the two new fixture cases carry live-checked jsonata-js reference values (node executed, nothing skipped); removing the alternative fails exactly the two named cases (reproduced); the whitespace-normalized alignment test bites (a divergent edit fails, reproduced).\n- Security: loopback-only binding, constant-time credential compare, 32KiB request and 16KiB payload bounds, 250ms query timeout, clamped wait timeout, SQL default-deny, signer custody, and frontier-wait behavior all verified unchanged. Minor record-only note: duplicate keys in the request envelope remain last-wins via the stock decoder; the winning payload is canonicalized and signed, so validated-bytes equals signed-bytes holds.\n- Gates, all run by the review at this head: gofmt clean, git diff --check clean, go build ok, go vet ok, spike package green under -race twice, full go test ./... -count=1 green with zero FAIL lines, exit 0.\n\nSimplification: the token-walking canonicalizer is near-minimal and its overlap with decodeEvent is acceptable defense-in-depth. The unsafeJSONata regex is at the edge of readability and is the wrong tool for a category guarantee — finding 2's AST-based fix and the simplification concern point the same direction.\n\nDo-not-merge respected. A repaired head answering the two findings returns to review.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:5f82618c206e97b6a3ca1fad8fd419b1c72ce5fb","head":"ebb4dceb7fbe5b4d422fedc85bb982522500247d","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:89bbeee5b5c1b7abd18a5c4521019c6d1f3b2a2d\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:0e4da430ba302d1c15e7a4c78c7912e60e0676bb","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"changes-requested"}}